------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Background

A Verkada gateway sits on a customer site next to a third-party access-control
and sensor bridge. The bridge is the only way to learn that a door opened, that
a camera saw motion, that someone pried a housing open, or that a sensor went
offline. Our software has to expose those events to everything else on the
gateway: the uploader, the local rules engine, the health reporter.

The vendor gives us three functions (evsrc.h):

    struct Event { int type; uint32_t device_id; uint64_t timestamp; };
        type       EV_MOTION / EV_DOOR / EV_TAMPER / EV_OFFLINE
        timestamp  microseconds, same clock as ev_now_us()

    int evsrc_read_blocking(struct Event *out);
        0   an event, stored in *out
       -1   transient vendor error, *out untouched, returns immediately
       -2   the call was cut short by evsrc_wake()

    void evsrc_wake(void);

  read_next behaviour, in detail:

    no timeout          There is no timeout argument and no non-blocking mode.
                        On a quiet night this call sits there for minutes.
    bursty              Events come in bursts: several within one millisecond,
                        then a gap. You cannot poll your way out of this.
    not thread-safe     The vendor keeps static state. Exactly ONE thread may
                        ever be inside evsrc_read_blocking().
    transient errors    -1 comes back instantly, so a bare retry loop spins a
                        core at 100%.
    evsrc_wake()        The one escape hatch, and you have to know to ask for
                        it: it makes a pending read return -2. It LATCHES —
                        after it is called every later read returns -2 too —
                        and there is no un-wake. It exists for shutdown.

  Time compression in the harness: real quiet periods are minutes; the fake
  vendor uses 20-200 ms gaps (and one forced 2.5 s quiet period during the
  shutdown test) so the whole run finishes in about 4 seconds.

Blocking, single-threaded and untimed is unusable for the rest of the gateway,
so wrap it in a non-blocking, thread-safe API.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Tasks

Part 1.  A non-blocking consumer API over a bounded queue.

      int  evq_init(void);
      void evq_deinit(void);
      int  evq_try_pop(struct Event *out);                        /* 0 / -1 */
      int  evq_pop_timed(struct Event *out, uint64_t timeout_us); /* 0 / -1 */
      void evq_stats(struct EvqStats *out);

    - try_pop never blocks and never touches the vendor.
    - pop_timed blocks at most timeout_us; use a condvar, not a sleep-and-poll
      loop. timeout_us == 0 must behave exactly like try_pop.
    - The queue is BOUNDED (EVQ_CAPACITY, 16 in the harness). On overflow the
      policy is explicit: drop the OLDEST event and count the drop in
      evq_stats(). Silent loss is the thing being tested against.
    - evq_deinit() must terminate cleanly even though the sampler thread is
      parked inside evsrc_read_blocking() with no timeout. How do you stop a
      thread that is blocked in a call that has no timeout? Answer that out
      loud before you write it. Also: what happens to a consumer that is
      asleep in evq_pop_timed() when you shut down? deinit must be idempotent.

Part 2.  Time-bucketed counting over the last 5 minutes.

      uint32_t evq_count_since(int type, uint64_t since_us);

    - "How many EV_TAMPER events since 90 seconds ago?" — the health reporter
      asks this every second, for every type.
    - Cost must be O(number of buckets), NOT O(number of events), and memory
      must not grow with the event rate. A tamper storm on eight doors is
      exactly when someone is querying.
    - Counted at ingest: an event still counts even if the queue dropped it.
      "What happened" and "what we processed" are different questions.
    - Resolution is one bucket wide: the answer includes the whole bucket that
      contains since_us. Rules: since_us in the future -> 0; since_us older
      than the window -> clamp to the window start; unknown type -> 0.
    - Reference answer: a ring of EVQ_NBUCKETS fixed-width buckets
      (300 x 1 s) with EV_TYPE_COUNT counters each, advanced LAZILY on insert
      (zero only the buckets you skipped, and memset the whole table if you
      skipped more than a lap). Insert O(1), query a bounded loop, memory
      fixed. ./main.sh fast rebuilds with 32 x 20 ms buckets so eviction and
      the lazy advance actually run inside a 4-second test.

